    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Most trials measure the first changes in skin hydration and elasticity at 4 to 8 weeks of daily collagen, with wrinkle and firmness changes closer to 8 to 12 weeks.</strong> Nail studies ran 24 weeks. Joint pain trials ran 12 to 24 weeks. Hair has the weakest evidence of all, and no trial has shown collagen alone regrows it.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Skin hydration and elasticity:</strong> measurable by week 4 to 6, clearest at week 12.</li>
        <li><strong>Wrinkle depth:</strong> 8 weeks minimum before the question is even fair.</li>
        <li><strong>Nails:</strong> 24 weeks in the one trial that measured growth rate.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> dermal collagen has a half-life of roughly 15 years, so nothing is being rebuilt in a fortnight.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

