    <div class="jr-prose">

      <h2 id="why-not-faster">Why doesn't collagen work in a week?</h2>
      <p>Because absorption and effect run on different clocks. The peptides reach your blood in an hour or two; the tissue they act on turns over across years.</p>
      <p>Human pharmacokinetic work found peptide-bound hydroxyproline rising about 30 minutes after a hydrolysed collagen dose, peaking at 1 to 2 hours, then halving by 4 hours. Delivery is not the bottleneck.</p>
      <p>The tissue is. Using aspartic acid racemisation as a clock, researchers put the half-life of collagen in human skin at 14.8 years, with a confidence interval of 9.4 to 22.3 years. The cross-linked triple helix resists being broken down, which is why skin collagen lasts, and why you cannot swap it out quickly.</p>
      <p>So what are trials measuring at week 8? Not replaced collagen, but new synthesis and hydration in the upper dermis, which move faster than the scaffold beneath. One trial found procollagen type I content 65% higher and elastin 18% higher than placebo at 8 weeks. The signal arrives first; the visible result lags.</p>

