      <h2 id="skin-timeline">How long does collagen take to work on skin?</h2>
      <p>Four to eight weeks for hydration and elasticity, eight to twelve for anything you would call a wrinkle change. Those windows hold across the better studies, and are where the evidence for oral <a href="/pages/collagen-peptides">collagen peptides</a> is strongest.</p>
      <p>The clearest dose-finding trial randomised 69 women to 2.5g, 5g or placebo daily for 8 weeks. Elasticity improved significantly at both the 4-week and 8-week marks, with no significant difference between doses. A second 2014 trial gave 114 women aged 45 to 65 a 2.5g dose for 8 weeks, and produced the procollagen and elastin figures above.</p>
      <p>The most useful study for timeline questions is a 2024 randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial, because it measured at week 6 as well as week 12. Hydration and elasticity beat placebo at both points. At 12 weeks it reported hydration up 13.8%, the R2 elasticity index up 22.7%, and collagen fragmentation down 44.6% on confocal microscopy.</p>
      <p>Pooled, a meta-analysis of 19 trials and 1,125 participants concluded that a 90-day course improves hydration and elasticity and reduces wrinkles. Ninety days is the number to hold.</p>
      <p>Two caveats, stated plainly. Most of these trials were funded by collagen manufacturers, and reviewers note that effects look weaker in higher-quality and non-industry studies. Several also combined collagen with vitamin C or other actives, so its effect cannot always be isolated. The direction is consistent; the size is probably smaller than the marketing implies.</p>

      <h2 id="timeline-table">The timeline by outcome</h2>
      <p>Collagen does not have one timeline. It has six, weeks to months apart.</p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
