      <p><strong>Nails.</strong> The study everyone cites gave 25 women with brittle nail syndrome 2.5g a day for 24 weeks. Growth rate rose 12% and broken nails fell 42%. It was open-label with no placebo arm, so treat 24 weeks as the honest wait and the effect size as provisional.</p>
      <p><strong>Joints.</strong> One trial gave 139 athletes with knee pain 5g daily for 12 weeks and found a significant drop in pain during activity, though resting pain did not separate from placebo. An earlier 24-week trial used 10g in 147 student athletes. If joints are your reason for taking it, <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a> covers the dosing properly.</p>
      <p><strong>Hair.</strong> I would rather disappoint you now than in month four. The 2024 trial above reported hair count up 27.6% at 12 weeks, but that result was not statistically significant, and the 2023 trials showing hair benefits combined collagen with iron, selenium and other amino acids. Hair is keratin, not collagen. <a href="/pages/collagen-for-hair">Collagen for hair</a> goes through what it can and cannot do.</p>

