      <h2 id="what-slows-it">Five things that stretch the timeline</h2>
      <p>When someone tells me collagen did nothing in three months, one of these five is involved.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>The dose was never in range.</strong> Trials used 2.5g to 10g daily. A collagen tablet typically holds under 1g, so reaching the low end means ten or more tablets a day. The arithmetic across formats is in <a href="/pages/collagen-tablets-vs-shots">collagen tablets vs shots</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>You skipped days.</strong> The 2024 trial dosed one arm daily and another every 48 hours, and the collagen-density and fragmentation gains appeared only in the daily arm. Consistency is the most evidence-backed variable here, and it beats every timing trick.</li>
        <li><strong>No vitamin C alongside it.</strong> Vitamin C is a required co-factor for the enzymes that hydroxylate proline and lysine during collagen synthesis. Collabloom carries 250mg of vitamin C from amla for exactly this reason.</li>
        <li><strong>Baseline protein was too low.</strong> A cereal-heavy vegetarian diet, common across India, often lands under the protein that skin and hair turnover needs, and a 30ml shot will not rescue a daily deficit. <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">Boosting collagen naturally</a> has the protein and vitamin C targets with numbers.</li>
        <li><strong>UV was undoing it faster than you built it.</strong> Ultraviolet exposure drives the enzyme activity that degrades dermal collagen, so in Indian sun an unprotected face can lose ground across the same twelve weeks you are supplementing. Sunscreen is what lets a collagen course show.</li>
      </ol>

