      <h2 id="when-to-stop">How long before you decide it isn't working?</h2>
      <p>Give it 12 weeks of genuinely daily use at a trial-range dose, then judge. Anything shorter and you are judging the wait, not the supplement.</p>
      <p>Three things make that judgement less of a guess. Photograph your face in the same light on day one and not again until week six, because daily mirror-checking cannot detect a 13% hydration change and will only talk you out of it. Track what you feel rather than see: tightness after cleansing, whether nails are still snapping. And change one variable at a time.</p>
      <p>If you have been consistent at a real dose for 12 weeks with vitamin C on board and nothing has shifted, collagen is not your limiting factor. That is useful, and it usually points somewhere more treatable: iron, thyroid, protein intake, sun exposure, or a diagnosis a supplement was never going to address.</p>

      <h2 id="see-a-doctor">When to see a doctor instead</h2>
      <p>Some things should not be given a twelve-week supplement trial, because the waiting is itself the harm. See a doctor rather than counting weeks if you have a widening parting or scalp visible through the hair, shedding that came on suddenly, a joint that swells, locks or gives way, nails that are lifting or changing colour, or a new or changing pigmented patch. Sudden diffuse shedding is often iron deficiency, a thyroid problem or a post-illness effect, and all three are confirmed with a blood test.</p>

