        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How long does collagen take to work on skin?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Hydration and elasticity move first, and trials detect them at 4 to 6 weeks of daily use. Wrinkle depth needs 8 weeks at minimum. A pooled analysis of 19 trials concluded that a 90-day course improves hydration, elasticity and wrinkles, so 12 weeks is the fair point to judge." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Can you see results from collagen in 2 weeks?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No trial has shown a measurable skin change at 2 weeks, and brands promising it are not citing evidence. The earliest instrument-measured changes in the published studies appear around week 4, so anything you notice in a fortnight is more likely to be fluid intake or a new skincare step started alongside it." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Is it bad to take collagen every other day?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "It appears meaningfully worse than daily. A 2024 randomised trial dosed one group daily and another every 48 hours, and the improvements in collagen density and fragmentation appeared only in the daily group. Every trial in this article dosed daily for 8 weeks or more." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How long does collagen take to work on hair?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "There is no reliable answer, because no trial has established that collagen alone changes hair. A 2024 trial reported hair count up 27.6% at 12 weeks, but the result was not statistically significant, and the 2023 studies showing benefits combined collagen with iron and selenium." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How long before collagen helps joint pain?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Twelve weeks in the trial that found an effect. Researchers gave 139 athletes with knee pain 5g daily for 12 weeks and saw a significant reduction in pain during activity, though pain at rest did not separate from placebo. An earlier trial used 10g for 24 weeks." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "What is the best time to take collagen, morning or night?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "It does not appear to matter. No trial has compared morning against evening, or with food against an empty stomach, so the empty-stomach advice you see repeated is theoretical. Take it whenever you will reliably remember every day, because daily consistency is the variable the evidence supports." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "How long should I take collagen before deciding it does not work?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Twelve weeks of genuinely daily use at 2.5g or more, with vitamin C alongside it. If nothing has changed by then, collagen is probably not your limiting factor, and the likelier culprits are protein intake, iron, thyroid function, sun exposure or an untreated skin condition." } },
        { "@type": "Question", "name": "Do the results last if I stop taking it?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Partly, and not indefinitely. In the nail study, 88% of participants still showed improvement four weeks after stopping, so the effect does not vanish immediately. But every trial measured people who were still supplementing, so benefit further out has not been demonstrated either way." } }
